Make PuTTY's option defaults survive a 64-bit build.

ConfKeyInfo stores each option's default in a union whose first member is the
one a positional initialiser fills. WinSCP put `int ival` first and writes
string defaults as `(int)"..."`, later read back through `sval`. That needs
pointers to fit in an int: on x86-64 it truncates every string default's
address, which clang rejects in a static initialiser and which would otherwise
crash at the first read (settings.c asserts on sval).

Make the first member pointer-sized and cast through intptr_t. Integer and
boolean defaults still read back correctly through ival and bval, on the
little-endian targets the port supports. Line counts are unchanged.

--- a/source/putty/putty.h	2026-10-11 13:07:45.400667374 -0300
+++ b/source/putty/putty.h	2026-10-11 13:07:45.429089797 -0300
@@ -1892,7 +1892,7 @@
     union {
         // WINSCP changed order, because only the first member can be initialized
         // and char* can be casted to int, but not to bool
-        int ival;
+        intptr_t ival; // linscp: pointer-sized, or string defaults truncate on 64-bit
         bool bval;
         const char *sval;
     } default_value;
